{"id": "section-body-29791", "title": "Sjögren’s Syndrome", "chapterId": "chapter-twenty-six", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "RHEUMATOLOGICAL DISORDERS", "Connective Tissue Diseases (CTDs)", "Sjögren’s Syndrome"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-29791", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Sjögren’s Syndrome", "runs": [{"text": "Sjögren’s"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Syndrome"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "105"}}, {"id": "body-29792", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Sjögren’s syndrome is a chronic autoimmune disease characterized by dry eyes (xeropthalmia) and dry mouth (xerostomia), resulting from lymphocytic destruction of the salivary and lacrimal glands. It may occur as a primary condition (primary Sjögren’s syndrome) or in association with other autoimmune diseases such as RA and SLE, viral infections such as HIV and Epstein– Barr virus, and primary biliary cirrhosis (secondary Sjögren’s syndrome).", "runs": [{"text": "Sjögren’s syndrome is a chronic autoimmune disease characterized by dry eyes (xeropthalmia) and dry mouth (xerostomia), resulting"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "from"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "lymphocytic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "destruction"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "salivary"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "lacrimal"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "glands."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "It"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "may"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "occur"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "as"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "a"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "primary"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "condition"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(primary"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Sjögren’s syndrome) or in association with other autoimmune diseases such as RA and SLE, viral infections such as HIV and Epstein– Barr virus, and primary biliary cirrhosis (secondary Sjögren’s syndrome)."}]}, {"id": "body-297